<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jakob Sprenger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1884">1884</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jakob Sprenger was a Nazi politician who served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a> of Hesse-Nassau and, from 1933, as Reich governor of the People's State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. He oversaw the Nazi takeover of the state's government and the persecution of its Jewish communities, among them the old communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> and Worms. He killed himself at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jakob-sprenger.jpg" alt="Jakob Sprenger" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sprenger-jakob-das-deutsche-ledermuseum-zum-25jaehrigen-Jubilaeum-im-kriegsjahr_1942-offenbach-graphische-werkstaette-rudolph-1942-vorsatz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sprenger, a farmer's son, was born on 24 July 1884 in Oberhausen in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-region">Rhenish Palatinate</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> in Bad Bergzabern and a year's volunteer service in the army, he joined the Imperial Postal Service in 1902, working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and from 1912 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>. He volunteered in August 1914, was wounded on the western front that November and awarded the Iron Cross 2nd Class, became a Leutnant in 1916 and served on the eastern front in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinsk">Pinsk</a> and Rivne before returning to the post office in Frankfurt in 1919.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> in 1922 and, while it was banned after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a>, led front groups in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>. Rejoining formally in August 1925, he rose quickly: he was elected to the Frankfurt City Council that year, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a> of Hesse-Nassau South on 1 April 1927 and entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> in September 1930, where he was the party's specialist on the civil service. He founded the Frankfurter Volksblatt. When his Gau was merged with Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> on 1 January 1933 he led the unified Gau Hesse-Nassau, and from 1 March 1935 he was also Minister-President of Hesse, taking over from Philipp Wilhelm Jung.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadamar">Hadamar</a> psychiatric clinic in his region became the Hadamar Killing Facility, where over 14,000 disabled men, women and children were murdered under Aktion T4 between January 1941 and March 1945, certainly with his knowledge. Over 10,600 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were deported from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and only about 600 Frankfurt Jews survived the war; in May 1943 Sprenger declared the city free of Jews.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In the war he was Reich Defense Commissioner for Military District XII, from 1944 Oberpräsident of the new Prussian Province of Nassau, uniting the highest party and state offices there, and commander of the Volkssturm in his Gau. On 15 March 1945, with American forces across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, he ordered that Germans who would not fight or wished to flee be shot or hanged. On 23 March he forbade able-bodied people to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, yet on the night of 25 to 26 March he fled to Kössen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>, where he and his wife took poison on 7 May 1945.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jakob Sprenger” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nazis took power in March 1933 under Reich governor <b>Jakob Sprenger</b>, and the Jewish communities of Mainz and Worms, among the oldest in Germany, were destroyed.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-state-of-hesse">People's State of Hesse</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Oberhausen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Kössen, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.1,8.06&amp;year=1923" aria-label="Oberhausen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4736 451 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jakob Sprenger"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5186 721h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5186 721h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.1,8.06&amp;year=1923">What was here in 1923? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
